          <a href="#by-problem" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Sizing by Problem</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost everyone who says an air purifier did nothing for them bought one rated for their room size. That rating assumes two air changes an hour, which is a general air-quality target. Allergy and smoke relief takes four to six. Size for the job rather than the square footage and the same category of product behaves completely differently.
        </p>

        <div id="two-numbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Two Numbers That Decide Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air purifier sizing rests on two figures, and only one of them is printed prominently on the box. Understanding both takes about a minute and it is the difference between a machine that works and a machine that hums in the corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>CADR</strong> — clean air delivery rate — is how much filtered air the unit produces, measured in cubic feet per minute and published separately for smoke, dust and pollen. It is an independently verified figure under the AHAM test programme, which makes it one of the few appliance specifications you can genuinely compare across brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>ACH</strong> — air changes per hour — is how many times the purifier can process the entire volume of your room in an hour. This is the number that determines whether you notice a difference, and it is the one manufacturers leave you to work out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The relationship is simple arithmetic. A room's volume is its floor area times the ceiling height. A purifier's hourly output is its CADR times sixty. Divide the second by the first and you have your air changes per hour.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Formula</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">ACH = (CADR &times; 60) &divide; (floor area &times; ceiling height)</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 250 CFM purifier in a 200 square foot room with 8-foot ceilings delivers (250 &times; 60) &divide; (200 &times; 8) = 9.4 air changes an hour on maximum. Run at the quiet speed you will actually use, that might be 3 to 4 — which is exactly the range you want for allergies.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That last sentence is the part people miss. CADR is measured at the highest fan speed, and nobody runs a purifier on maximum in a bedroom. Realistic sizing assumes you will use a middle or low setting, which is why buying a unit rated for roughly twice your room is not overkill but ordinary practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sizing by Room, Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            These figures target four to five air changes an hour at a moderate fan speed, which is the level at which people with allergies consistently report a difference. They assume standard 8-foot ceilings.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dust CADR Needed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Room Rating on the Box</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100-150 sq ft (bedroom)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110-160 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250-350 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150-250 sq ft (large bedroom)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160-260 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">350-550 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">250-400 sq ft (living room)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">260-420 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">550-900 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400-600 sq ft (open plan)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">420-620 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">900-1,300 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">600+ sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two units</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Do not rely on one machine</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the third column carefully. To get allergy-level air changes in a 200 square foot bedroom, you are shopping among purifiers marketed for rooms two to three times that size. That is not a trick — the box rating is calculated for two air changes an hour, which is a perfectly reasonable target for general air quality and about half of what allergy relief takes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical shortcut, if you would rather not do arithmetic in a shop: <strong>buy a dust CADR figure roughly equal to your room's square footage</strong>. A 250 square foot room wants about 250 CFM. It is a rule of thumb rather than physics, but it lands close to five air changes an hour in a standard-ceilinged room and it has never steered anyone badly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-ratings-mislead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why the Room Rating on the Box Misleads
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturers are not being dishonest. The standard room-size claim is derived from CADR using an assumption of two air changes per hour, and for general air quality — reducing background dust in a house with no particular problem — two is a sensible target.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mismatch is that almost nobody buys an air purifier for general air quality. People buy them because of pollen, a cat, wildfire smoke, a musty basement or a new baby, and every one of those is a case where the accepted target is four, five or six changes an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second, quieter problem with the rating: it is measured at maximum fan speed in a sealed chamber. Your room has a door that opens, a window that leaks, furniture that blocks airflow and a fan setting you chose because the top speed was too loud to sleep beside. Every one of those pushes real-world performance below the laboratory number.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes CADR a bad specification — it is the best one in the category precisely because it is independently verified. It just has to be read as a ceiling rather than as a promise, and converted into air changes for the room you actually have.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing worth checking while you are reading the box: the three CADR figures are usually different. Smoke CADR is measured against the smallest particles and is therefore the most demanding; pollen CADR against the largest. If your problem is smoke or fine particulates, size on the smoke figure rather than the headline one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ceiling-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ceilings, Open Plans and the Multiplier Nobody Applies
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every sizing chart in this category, including ours, assumes 8-foot ceilings. Rooms with higher ceilings hold proportionally more air, and the purifier has to process all of it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceiling Height</h3>

          <p>
            A 10-foot ceiling adds 25 percent to the volume of the room; a 12-foot ceiling adds 50 percent. Multiply your required CADR accordingly. This is the single most common reason a correctly sized purifier underperforms in a converted loft, a barn conversion or a room with a vaulted ceiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Open Plan Space</h3>

          <p>
            Air does not respect the boundary where your kitchen becomes your living room. If the space is genuinely open, size for the whole connected volume rather than the part you are standing in. A purifier rated for the sofa end of a 600 square foot open plan is simply too small, and it will run at maximum permanently without reaching the target.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Doors and the Rest of the House</h3>

          <p>
            The corollary is a genuinely useful piece of advice: <strong>close the door</strong>. A purifier in a closed bedroom is treating 1,200 cubic feet. The same purifier with the door open is trying to treat the whole floor, and it will never get there. This is why the bedroom is the right place for a single purifier and why it works far better than the same machine in a hallway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-or-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. One Large Unit or Two Smaller Ones?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a single closed room, one appropriately sized unit is better — fewer plugs, less noise, lower cost, one filter to replace. For a home, two smaller purifiers in the two rooms you actually occupy beat one large machine every time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is that air mixes poorly between rooms. A powerful purifier in a hallway produces clean air in the hallway, and the closed bedroom where you spend eight hours breathing is barely affected. Coverage matters more than capacity once you are dealing with more than one room.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Better Choice</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One bedroom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One right-sized unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheaper, quieter, one filter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom plus living room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two units</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Air does not cross closed doors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large open plan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two units, spread apart</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faster mixing, lower noise each</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole house, forced-air HVAC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">MERV 13 filter plus one room unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The blower is already moving air everywhere</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That last row is worth taking seriously if you have ducted heating or cooling. Upgrading the HVAC filter to MERV 13 turns the system into a whole-house purifier whenever the blower runs, for a fraction of what several standalone units cost. Check that your system tolerates the pressure drop first — some older furnaces do not — and pair it with a single good unit in the bedroom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise Is a Sizing Constraint, Not a Comfort Feature
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part of sizing that no chart captures and that decides more outcomes than any other. A purifier only cleans air while it is switched on, and a machine too loud to sleep beside gets switched off at exactly the hours it matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Typical figures run from about 24 dB on the lowest setting to 55-65 dB on maximum. The low setting is what you will use overnight, and it typically delivers somewhere between a third and half of the rated CADR.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which leads directly to the sizing rule: <strong>choose a purifier whose low or medium speed meets your target, not its maximum</strong>. Practically, that means buying a unit rated for roughly twice your room, so the quiet setting alone does the work. An oversized purifier on low is quieter, cleans the same amount of air, and lasts longer than a correctly sized one running flat out.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth checking the noise figure at a stated fan speed rather than trusting a single headline number. A machine advertising 24 dB is quoting its lowest setting, which may move very little air. The useful comparison is decibels at the speed that delivers the CADR you need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sizing by the Problem You Are Solving
          </h2>

          <p>
            The target air change rate depends on what you are trying to remove, and the difference between the low and high end is a factor of three in the size of machine you need.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Problem</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Target ACH</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sizing Approach</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">General air quality</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The box rating is fine as printed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dust and pet dander</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">CADR roughly equal to square footage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pollen and seasonal allergies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same, and run it continuously</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wildfire or wood smoke</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Size on smoke CADR, go one size up</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mould spores and musty odour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs real activated carbon weight too</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cooking odours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carbon capacity matters more than CADR</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Smoke is the demanding case. The particles are small, they arrive faster than a purifier removes them while an event is ongoing, and six or more air changes an hour is the accepted target. In wildfire season that means sizing generously and sealing the room — a purifier cannot outrun an open window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Odours and gases are a different problem entirely, and CADR does not measure them. Removing cooking smells, volatile compounds or the musty note of a damp basement depends on the weight of activated carbon in the filter, not on airflow. A machine with a token carbon-coated mesh will not do it however large its CADR. If odour is your reason for buying, look for a purifier that states the carbon weight in pounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the problem, the final check is the same: work out the air changes per hour your chosen machine delivers at the fan speed you will genuinely leave it running on. If that number is at or above the target in the table, the purifier is the right size. If it is not, no feature on the box will compensate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying to the Room Rating on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                That figure is calculated for two air changes an hour, which is a general air-quality target and roughly half what allergy or smoke relief requires. Buy a unit rated for about twice your room, or size on CADR directly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Ceiling Height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every sizing chart assumes 8-foot ceilings. A 12-foot ceiling adds 50 percent to the room's volume and therefore to the CADR you need. It is the usual explanation for a correctly sized purifier underperforming in a loft or vaulted room.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing for Maximum Fan Speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CADR is measured flat out, and nobody sleeps next to a purifier running flat out. Size so that the low or medium setting meets your target, which in practice means buying one size up from the arithmetic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting One Large Unit in a Hallway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Air mixes poorly between rooms and barely at all through a closed door. Two smaller purifiers in the two rooms you occupy will outperform one powerful machine in the corridor between them, every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting CADR to Handle Odours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CADR measures particle removal, not gases. Cooking smells, volatile compounds and musty odours are removed by activated carbon, and the specification that matters is carbon weight — a thin carbon-coated mesh does almost nothing regardless of airflow.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air purifier do I need for a bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical 150 to 250 square foot bedroom, look for a dust CADR of 160 to 260 CFM, which usually means a unit marketed for 350 to 550 square feet. That delivers four to five air changes an hour at a moderate fan speed — the level at which allergy sufferers consistently notice a difference — rather than the two the box rating assumes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate what size air purifier I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply floor area by ceiling height to get room volume, multiply CADR by 60 to get hourly output, then divide the second by the first for air changes per hour. Aim for four to five for allergies, six or more for smoke. The shortcut is to buy a dust CADR figure roughly equal to your room's square footage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is CADR and why does it matter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean air delivery rate measures how much filtered air a purifier produces, in cubic feet per minute, verified independently under the AHAM programme. It is the only genuinely comparable performance figure in the category. It is published separately for smoke, dust and pollen, and smoke is the most demanding of the three.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to buy an air purifier that is too big?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within reason. An oversized unit run on its quiet setting delivers the same clean air as a correctly sized one running at maximum, while being far quieter and less stressed. Since the quiet setting is the one you will actually use overnight, buying a size up is the standard advice rather than an extravagance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need one air purifier per room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the rooms you spend real time in, yes. Air mixes poorly between rooms and hardly at all through a closed door, so one large machine in a hallway leaves the bedroom largely untreated. If you buy one purifier, put it in the bedroom and close the door — that is where the uninterrupted hours are.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does ceiling height change what size purifier I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Considerably. Sizing charts assume 8-foot ceilings, so a 10-foot ceiling adds 25 percent to the volume and a 12-foot ceiling adds 50 percent. Multiply your required CADR by the same proportion, or the machine will run permanently at maximum without ever reaching the target air change rate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air purifier do I need for smoke?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Size on the smoke CADR figure rather than the headline number, target six or more air changes an hour, and go one size larger than the arithmetic suggests. Smoke particles are small and, during an active event, arrive continuously — so seal the room as well, because no purifier outruns an open window.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

